| D2 | The module lives in this repo at `src/eligibility/`, with lint-enforced boundaries, so it can be copied into a new service or extracted into a package later. | 2026-10-06 |
| D3 | Answers stay in the session until there is a result. The host decides what to save at that point. | 2026-10-06 |
| D4 | Branching rules are ported as plain TypeScript (a port of CCQ's `Steps::Logic` and section classes). Forge handles navigation only. | Proposed |
| D5 | Session answer keys use CCQ's names exactly (for example `partner_` prefixes and add-another list keys), to make parity testing straightforward. | Proposed |

## Background

### Forge in this app

- The app uses `@ministryofjustice/hmpps-forge` (^0.4.4). Journeys are packaged with `createForgePackage({ journey, functions })` and registered in `src/app.ts` with `forge.registerPackage(pkg, deps)`. Dependencies are injected at that point.
- Forge does **not** keep answers between requests. On POST, answers come from the request body. Anything that must persist has to be written by our own effects (functions the journey runs when a step is accessed or submitted).
- The existing journeys are tied to this app in several ways. The eligibility module must not be:
  - SiLAS auth (`requireSilasAuth`)
  - `/case/:caseId` parameters
  - `ctx.getState("authenticatedAxios")`
  - `/call_centre/api/v1/` paths
  - app layouts
  - English text hard-coded in `block.ts` files

### The CCQ journey

CCQ is in `~/dev/laa-check-client-qualifies`; this summary was taken at commit `1a30ea3f`.

- **Step list:** the step-to-form map is in `app/controllers/flow/handler.rb`. The step list is rebuilt from the session on every request, from six sections in a fixed order: NonFinancial, Income, Partner, Outgoings, Property, AssetsAndVehicles (`app/lib/steps/helper.rb:72-79`).
- **Branching:** all of it comes from one set of predicates in `app/lib/steps/logic.rb`. The main ones are `controlled?`, `client_under_eighteen?`, `under_eighteen_no_means_test_required?`, `immigration_or_asylum?`, `asylum_supported?`, `non_means_tested?`, `passported?`, `skip_income_questions?`, `skip_capital_questions?` and `partner?`.
- **Size:** there are 41 possible steps. Six of them are add-another lists on a single page (incomes, benefits, dependant incomes, additional properties, bank accounts, vehicles).
- **Early exits:**
  - "Cannot use this service": shared ownership where the landlord is not the only other joint owner, or additional property in shared ownership.
  - An early gross-income result after `other_income`.
- **Check your answers:** has a change loop. Edits go into a pending copy that is committed when the loop ends, and the user is walked through any steps that become relevant.
- **CFE (Check Financial Eligibility) calls:**
  - `POST v6/assessments` for the assessment
  - `GET state_benefit_type` for benefit autocomplete

  About ten builder services assemble the payload from the relevant steps only. The result is `eligible`, `contribution_required` or `ineligible`.
- **Welsh:** there is no Welsh UI. Only the downloadable controlled work (CW) PDF forms have Welsh versions.

#### Step order

| # | Step | Shown when |
|---|---|---|
| 1 | `client_age` | Always |
| 2 | `level_of_help` | Always |
| 3–7 | `under_18_clr`, `aggregated_means`, `how_to_aggregate`, `regular_income`, `under_eighteen_assets` | Controlled work and under 18. A chain: any "yes" ends it |
| 8 | `domestic_abuse_applicant` | Certificated work and means-tested |
| 9–10 | `immigration_or_asylum`, `immigration_or_asylum_type` | Controlled work |
| 11 | `immigration_or_asylum_type_upper_tribunal` | Certificated work and not a domestic abuse applicant |
| 12 | `asylum_support` | Immigration or asylum matter |
| 13 | `applicant` (partner, passporting benefit) | Means-tested |
| 14–16 | `dependant_details`, `dependant_income`, `dependant_income_details` | Means-tested and not passported |
| 17–21 | `employment_status`, `income`, `benefits`, `benefit_details`, `other_income` | Not skipping income questions |
| 22–27 | `partner_details`, `partner_employment_status`, `partner_income`, `partner_benefits`, `partner_benefit_details`, `partner_other_income` | Has a partner. If passported, only `partner_details` |
| 28–29 | `outgoings`, `partner_outgoings` | Not skipping income questions |
| 30–32 | `property`, `property_landlord`, and one of the housing-cost steps | Not skipping capital questions. Housing costs depend on ownership type |
| 33–37 | `property_entry`, `additional_property`(`_details`), `partner_additional_property`(`_details`) | Not skipping capital questions |
| 38–39 | `assets`, `partner_assets` | Not skipping capital questions |
| 40–41 | `vehicle`, `vehicles_details` | Certificated work only |

The full predicate code is in `app/lib/steps/logic.rb`, and each section's rules are in `app/lib/steps/*_section.rb`.

#### Out of scope for the module

These CCQ features belong to individual services, not the shared journey:

- CW form PDFs
- LAA Portal SAML sign-in
- analytics
- the journey logger
- result PDF download

A host can provide its own versions through the `ResultHandler` hook described below.

## Design

### Module layout

```
src/eligibility/                 ← the reusable unit: copy this folder to reuse
  index.ts                       ← public API; the only file hosts may import
  README.md                      ← how to mount, what to provide, options
  domain/
    logic.ts                     ← port of Steps::Logic
    sections/*.ts                ← port of the six section classes → stepsFor(answers)
    navigation.ts                ← nextStep, previousStep, relevantAnswers, cannotUseService
    cfe/*.ts                     ← port of the CFE payload builders → buildAssessment(answers)
    result.ts                    ← port of CfeResult / CalculationResult
  ports/                         ← AnswerStore, Assessor, ResultHandler interfaces
  adapters/                      ← SessionAnswerStore, CfeHttpClient
  journey/                       ← createEligibilityPackage(opts); one folder per step
  registries/                    ← Eligibility.* effects and conditions
  views/eligibility/*.njk        ← extend a layout supplied by the host
  locales/en.json
  tests/

src/journeys/eligibility-host/   ← cla-operator-app specific; NOT copied
  index.ts                       ← mounts the module with SiLAS auth, case param, layout
  resultHandler.ts               ← saves the result against the case
```

### Public API

```ts
const eligibilityPkg = createEligibilityPackage({
  path: "eligibility",                         // relative to the parent journey
  onAccess: [requireSilasAuth],                // host's auth guard
  checkId: Params("caseId"),                   // how this host identifies a check
  presetAnswers: { level_of_help: "controlled" }, // steps fully answered here are skipped
  onComplete: Format("/case/%1/eligibility-result", Params("caseId")),
  template: { layout: "layouts/operator.njk" },
});

forge.registerPackage<EligibilityDeps>(eligibilityPkg, {
  assessor: new CfeHttpClient(config.cfe),
  resultHandler: new CaseResultHandler(apiService),
  // answerStore defaults to SessionAnswerStore
});
```

```ts
interface EligibilityDeps {
  assessor: Assessor;           // assess(payload), stateBenefitTypes()
  resultHandler: ResultHandler; // handle({ checkId, answers, result })
  answerStore?: AnswerStore;    // load(checkId), save(checkId, partial), clear(checkId)
}
```

### Boundary rules

- Code in `src/eligibility/` may only import:
  - `@ministryofjustice/hmpps-forge/*`
  - Node built-ins
  - its own files

  That rules out `#src/*`, `#utils/*`, `#types/*`, and any `../` path that leaves the folder. An ESLint `no-restricted-imports` override scoped to the folder enforces this.
- Host code may only import from `src/eligibility/index.ts`. A second lint rule enforces this.
- Auth, check identity, storage, the CFE client, layout and completion are all injected through `createEligibilityPackage` options or `EligibilityDeps`.
- All user-facing text uses keys from `src/eligibility/locales/en.json`. These are ported from CCQ's `config/locales/en.yml`, including the variants by level of help and for a partner.

### How Forge is used

- The journey runs `Eligibility.LoadAnswers` on access. It loads **every** saved answer into Forge with `setAnswer`, and publishes a navigation map (`Data("nav")`, step code to next step) built from `stepsFor(answers)`. See spike S1 for why both are needed.
- Each step redirects with `redirect({ goto: Data("nav.<step code>") })`. Forge's reachability checking then follows the same map, so a step that `stepsFor(answers)` leaves out cannot be reached.
- A valid submit runs `Eligibility.SaveAnswers`, which saves the answers and rebuilds the map before the redirect is evaluated.
- The journey sets `reachability: { unreachableRedirect: "frontier" }`, so a user who opens a step too early is sent to the first unanswered relevant step.
- If `cannotUseService(answers)` is true after a submit, the user is redirected to the module's "cannot use this service" page.
- Check your answers submits to `Eligibility.RunAssessment`, which does the following in order:
  1. builds the payload from `relevantAnswers(answers)`
  2. calls `assessor.assess`
  3. calls `resultHandler.handle`
  4. clears the session answers
  5. redirects to `onComplete`
- Add-another lists use the MOJ Frontend add-another component, subject to spike S2.

### Session storage

- Answers are stored under `req.session.eligibility[checkId]`, so checks in different tabs do not collide.
- If CFE fails, answers stay in the session and the user can retry.
- The change-answers loop keeps a pending copy at `req.session.eligibility[checkId].pending`, as CCQ does. It is committed only when the loop completes.

### cla-operator-app integration

- Mount the module as a child of `caseJourney` (`src/journeys/inbound-call/journey.ts`) at `/case/:caseId/eligibility/...`. It inherits `LoadCase` and receives SiLAS auth through `onAccess`.
- Other changes outside the module:
  - register the package in `src/journeys/index.ts`
  - add `src/eligibility/views` to `utils/nunjucksSetup.ts`
  - merge `src/eligibility/locales` in `i18nLoader.ts`
  - add `src/eligibility/**/*.spec.ts` to `.mocharc.json`
  - make sure the Docker image ships `src/eligibility/views` and `locales`, since templates are read from disk at runtime
- End-to-end tests stay in `tests/playwright/`, with CFE stubbed through MSW.

## Delivery phases

### Phase 0: spikes (about 2 to 3 days)

| Spike | Question to answer |
|---|---|
| S1 | Does Forge's reachability checking work with a runtime-computed `goto`? If not, use `entryWhen` with an `Eligibility.StepIsRelevant(code)` condition, or disable reachability checks for this journey. |
| S2 | Do MOJ add-another lists post nested arrays that Forge can validate item by item (possibly with `CollectionBlock`)? |
| S3 | Is there a network route from this app's namespace to `cfe-civil`? Add `CFE_HOST` to config. |
| S4 | Do session writes from effects persist across POST then redirect? |

Spike tests live in `src/eligibility/tests/forge-assumptions/` and run with the unit tests. If a Forge upgrade breaks an assumption the design depends on, they fail.

#### S1 findings: runtime-computed goto (2026-10-06)

**Result: works.** Forge 0.4.4's reachability checking accepts a `goto` computed at runtime. Test: `s1-dynamic-goto.spec.ts`.

How it works: for each request, Forge evaluates every step's redirect `goto` after the access phase, then walks forward from the entry step to decide which steps can be reached. A computed target is therefore treated like a static one, as long as the data it reads is in place before reachability runs.

The design depends on three conditions:

1. **Load every saved answer on access, not just the current step's.** Forge only follows a step's `goto` if that step's own answers are valid. If earlier answers are not loaded, every later step is unreachable. A control run without loading showed this: GET on any step after the first redirected back.
2. **Store the next step for every step**, as `Data("nav.<step code>")`, not as a single `Data("nextStep")`. Reachability evaluates all steps' `goto` in the same request, so one shared value would point every step at the same place.
3. **Rebuild the map in the submit effect.** On POST the access phase runs before the posted answer is read, so `SaveAnswers` must recompute `nav` before the redirect is evaluated. Forge evaluates `onValid` effects before `next`, so this works.

Behaviour confirmed:

- The posted answer chooses the branch: under 18 goes to the conditional step, otherwise the conditional step is skipped.
- GET on a later step renders when the saved answers make it reachable.
- GET on a step that the answers make irrelevant redirects.
- When an earlier answer changes, steps on the abandoned branch become unreachable. Their stale answers remain in the session, as in CCQ, so `relevantAnswers` must filter them before building the CFE payload.
- With `unreachableRedirect: "frontier"`, a user who opens a step too early goes to the first unanswered relevant step. With the default, they go back to the first step.

No fallback (`entryWhen` with a condition, or disabling reachability checks) is needed.

#### S4 findings: session persistence (2026-10-06)

**Result: works.** Test: `s4-session-persistence.spec.ts`. It uses a real Express app with `express-session` (same options as `config.session`, default `MemoryStore`), Forge's `createExpressRouter`, and supertest.

- Forge's Express adapter passes `req.session` to effects by reference (`context.getSession()`). Changes made by an effect are saved by `express-session` before the redirect response is sent.
- Confirmed:
  - after a POST, the next step renders on the following GET (it is only reachable if the answer was saved)
  - going back pre-fills the saved answer
  - answers build up across several steps
  - a different browser session does not see the answers
  - two checks in one browser session, keyed by `checkId` (`req.session.eligibility[checkId]`), stay separate
- A control run with a fresh session object on each request failed as expected.
- Effects must change the object returned by `getSession()` in place. The effect context has no way to replace the session.
- This does not change the `MemoryStore` risk below. Answers are still lost on a restart or if requests go to a different pod.

#### S3 findings: network route to CFE (2026-10-06)

**Result: no route yet.** This app cannot call CFE inside the cluster until the CFE team allows it.

Checked against a local copy of `cloud-platform-environments` from 18 September 2026. Check again against `main` before raising requests.

- Each `cfe-civil-*` namespace has an `allow-source-namespace` network policy listing which namespaces may call it. No `laa-cla-operator-app-*` namespace is listed.
- cla_backend already calls CFE inside the cluster (`CFE_HOST` in `cla_backend/helm_deploy`):

  | cla_backend environment | CFE it calls |
  |---|---|
  | uat | `http://cfe-civil.cfe-civil-staging.svc.cluster.local` |
  | staging | `http://cfe-civil.cfe-civil-production.svc.cluster.local` |
  | training | `http://cfe-civil.cfe-civil-production.svc.cluster.local` |
  | production | `http://cfe-civil.cfe-civil-production.svc.cluster.local` |

  Staging and training use **production** CFE.
- In the same copy, `laa-cla-backend-staging`'s policy does not list `laa-cla-operator-app-staging` either, and there is no `laa-cla-operator-app-training` namespace. Either the copy is out of date, or this app reaches cla_backend another way. Check before relying on either.
- Calling CFE directly needs a pull request to `cloud-platform-environments` adding each `laa-cla-operator-app-*` namespace to the matching `cfe-civil-*` policy, approved by the CFE team. Going through cla_backend needs no network change. This affects Q4.

Done in this spike:

- Added `config.cfe.baseUrl`, read from `CFE_HOST`, to `config.ts` and `types/config-types.ts`.
- Added `CFE_HOST` to `.env.example`, set to the public staging URL that CCQ uses for local development.
- Helm values are **not** set yet. Add them once Q4 is answered and the network policy is in place. If calling directly, match cla_backend: `cfe-civil-staging` for uat, and `cfe-civil-production` for staging, training and production, unless the CFE team prefers otherwise.

### Phase 1: rules engine and parity tests (no UI)

- Port `logic.rb`, the section classes, the CFE payload builders and the result interpretation into `domain/`.
- Port CCQ's RSpec cases for step logic and payload builders into Mocha tables.
- Build a parity fixture set from CCQ's FactoryBot "instant" sessions. The test is: the same answers must give the same step list and the same CFE payload JSON as CCQ.

### Phase 2: first end-to-end journey

- Build the module skeleton, the lint boundaries, `SessionAnswerStore` and `CfeHttpClient`.
- Implement these steps:
  - the non-financial section
  - `applicant`
  - the passported route through property and assets, including the bank accounts list
- Add check your answers and a basic result page.
- Mount the module in cla-operator-app.

### Phase 3: remaining sections

- Income, partner, dependants, outgoings, housing costs, additional property, vehicles.
- The "cannot use this service" page.
- Benefit autocomplete from CFE.

### Phase 4: CCQ's more detailed behaviour

- The change-answers loop.
- The early gross-income result.
- Copy that varies by level of help and for a partner.
- Childcare eligibility within the outgoings step.

## Risks

| Risk | Mitigation |
|---|---|
| Sessions use express-session `MemoryStore` (`utils/session.ts`), which keeps each session in one pod's memory. Staging runs 2 replicas and production 4, with no session affinity (found in spike S3), so this already affects the app, not just this journey: a request sent to a different pod loses the session, including SiLAS sign-in. | Move to a shared store such as Redis (`connect-redis`) before the eligibility journey goes live, or add sticky sessions as a stopgap. Track as a separate app-wide ticket. |
| Forge is pre-1.0 (0.4.x), so its API may change. | Pin the version. Keep Forge-specific code in `journey/` and `registries/` so `domain/` is unaffected. |
| Forge declares Node 20/22/24 support, but this app runs Node 26. | Confirm with the Forge maintainers, or test explicitly. |
| A second copy of Forge (`ts-app/node_modules`) could cause duplicate-instance bugs if bundled. | Remove the stray `ts-app/`. List Forge as a peer dependency if the module is extracted. |
| Parity drift: CCQ changes after the port. | Pin the CCQ commit the port is based on (currently `1a30ea3f`). Re-run parity fixtures when CCQ changes. |
| The create-case redirect bug (`search-client/step.ts:66` resolves to `/receive-call/cases/<ref>`) makes `/case/:caseId` unreachable. | Fix before mounting the eligibility journey under the case journey. |

